.TH FILE 1
.SH NAME
file \- guess a file's type from its content, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B file
[
.B -m
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I File
is a structural Limbo translation of Plan 9's real
.IR file (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/file.c ,
1772 lines in the 9front tree), not a behavioral workalike reverse-
engineered from a host tool \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree for the distinction, and
.B file.b 's
own header comment for the fuller derivation this page summarises. It
lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I File
reads each
.I file
argument (or standard input, if none are given), and tries roughly
thirty independent classifiers against its content in a fixed order,
printing the description the first one to match reports. A directory
is reported as such without being read; an empty file is reported as
empty. With multiple
.I file
arguments, each result is preceded by the argument's own name, name
columns aligned to the widest argument given.
.SS Options
.TP
.B -m
Print a MIME type instead of a human-readable description.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/file.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/file.c )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR filetest (1)
.SH BUGS
Two classifiers are not ported: the original's
.BR isexec ()
and
.BR iscint ()
recognise native Plan 9 executables and compiler/assembler
intermediate files by calling
.BR crackhdr ()/ objtype ()
from Plan 9's libmach. libmach is not absent from this tree \(em it
exists at
.BR utils/libmach ,
built with the kencc toolchain \(em but it is host-native C with no
Limbo/Dis binding, and porting an entire executable-header-cracking
library is a different, much larger undertaking than this file.
Dropping these two is a real behaviour change:
.B isexec
was 4th in the original's own dispatch order, so a native Plan 9
executable that would have been precisely identified there instead
falls through every remaining classifier, typically landing on the
entropy check
.RB ( ismung ())
or the final generic "binary" line.
.PP
The original gates most files behind a check that
.B mbuf->type
is a Plan 9 mounted-file or pipe channel type
.RB ( 'M' / '|' ),
treating anything else as a "special file" it refuses to read. That
check does not transfer: this emu's own host-filesystem-backed files
were checked directly (a real file, stat()ed and its
.B dtype
printed) and report
.B 'U' ,
not
.BR 'M' \(em
porting the exact character check would misclassify every ordinary
file here as special and skip analysis entirely. Adapted rather than
preserved: only directories are special-cased; everything else is read
and classified regardless of its
.BR dtype .
.PP
A real, preserved bug, found by testing this translation rather than
by reading the source: the word-lookup table
.B dict[]
requires binary search, which requires the table to be sorted \(em and
it is not. The word
.B "bio"
sits at its own position in the original (right after
.BR "extern" ),
but belongs six entries earlier, between
.B "block"
and
.BR "char" .
The result: a real search over the table as written silently fails to
find
.BR "data" ", " "dimension" ", " "double" ", " "extern" ", "
and
.BR "bio"
even though all five are present \(em confirmed by simulating the
exact search against the actual table order, not assumed. A C source
file's own
.B "#include <bio.h>"
line is not counted toward the include-detection heuristic as a
result; the other two heuristics
.RI ( isc ()'s
own declaration- and assignment-shaped checks) can still recognise
such a file, so this narrows one detection path rather than breaking C
recognition outright. Preserved as-is (a silent miscount, not a
dangerous one) rather than sorted correctly, per this tree's
translate-what's-there discipline; see
.IR filetest (1)'s
own case built specifically around this.
.PP
Content is read and matched as
.B array of byte
throughout, not
.B string
\(em the opposite choice from every other translation in
.BR p9/cmd .
.IR file 's
whole job is sniffing raw bytes, and several of its own magic tables
contain values that are not valid UTF-8 on their own (a PNG's leading
.B 0x89
byte, gzip's
.BR 0x1f\ 0x9d ,
the raw
.B 0xff Ns -heavy
Xilinx bitstream patterns); round-tripping those through a Limbo
string would corrupt them, since converting a string back to bytes
re-encodes each character as UTF-8 rather than returning the original
byte.
.PP
Plan 9's
.BR chartorune ()
and Inferno's
.B sys->byte2char()
disagree about what an invalid byte sequence decodes to \(em Plan 9
returns the raw byte value (landing it in the classifier's own
"invalid runes" bucket), Inferno returns the literal constant
.B Runeerror
.RB ( 0xFFFD ),
confirmed by reading
.BR libinterp/runt.c 's
own implementation, not assumed. The character-frequency histogram
here substitutes the raw byte value for
.B Runeerror
to match Plan 9's own convention, since the classifiers built on top
of it depend on that specific bucketing.
.PP
Three classifiers
.RB ( longoff (),
.IR isoffstr (),
.IR istar ())
seek the open file descriptor (to 256K, 32K, and 0 respectively) and
never restore the original position afterward \(em faithful to the C,
which does the same, and harmless here because
.B buf
is already fully read before any classifier runs, so nothing
downstream re-reads from
.BR fd .
Noted so a future classifier added after these in the dispatch order
does not silently assume the descriptor is still at its start.
.PP
.IR istar ()'s
checksum field parser accepts any of the three real-world tar
checksum-field layouts (6 octal digits + NUL + space, 7 digits + NUL,
6 digits + space + NUL) by the same logic the original's
.B strtol(hdr->chksum,\ 0,\ 8)
uses \(em skip leading whitespace, stop at the first non-octal
character \(em but only one layout is exercised by
.IR filetest (1)'s
own hand-built tar header; see that page's BUGS.
